<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 17" label="MODULE 17 / SMALL NETWORK DESIGN AND TROUBLESHOOTING" /><h1>Build the network.<br /><span>Prove it works.</span></h1><p>A small network combines endpoint devices, switches, routers, wireless access, addressing, and application services. A reliable build includes a clear plan and a repeatable verification process.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Plan the small network</h2><p>Identify the required users, applications, devices, addressing, wireless coverage, Internet access, and security needs. Select suitable switches, routers, access points, and media. Use a logical topology to document VLANs, subnets, interfaces, and default gateways, and a physical topology to document device placement and cabling.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1419 · IMG-1399 · IMG-1472</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Scale without losing control</h2><p>As a network grows, use hierarchical design, structured addressing, documented configurations, and consistent security policy. Plan for additional users, services, and traffic. Standardized naming, configuration backups, and monitoring make a network easier to maintain and troubleshoot.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1426 · IMG-1413</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Troubleshoot methodically</h2><p>Define the problem, gather evidence, consider likely causes, test one hypothesis at a time, implement the fix, and verify full operation. Start with physical link and interface status, then check IP addressing, subnet masks, default gateways, routing, name resolution, and application services. Record the cause and resolution so the same fault is easier to diagnose next time.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1428 · IMG-1407 · IMG-1423 · IMG-1466 · IMG-1468 · IMG-1481 · IMG-1482</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-lab"><div class="lesson-number">LAB</div><div><h2>Follow a repeatable troubleshooting path</h2><p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A user can reach a public IP address but cannot open a website by name. Start with the endpoint and move outward.</p><ol><li>On Windows, run <code>ipconfig /all</code>. Check the IPv4 address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS server. An address in <code>169.254.0.0/16</code> suggests the host did not obtain a DHCP lease.</li><li>Run <code>ping 127.0.0.1</code> to test the local TCP/IP stack, then ping the host's own address and default gateway.</li><li>Run <code>ping 1.1.1.1</code>. If this succeeds but names fail, basic IP connectivity is working and DNS becomes a strong suspect.</li><li>Run <code>nslookup example.com</code> to query the configured DNS server. Check the server address and whether the query times out or returns an incorrect result.</li><li>Use <code>tracert 1.1.1.1</code> to inspect the path if remote connectivity is failing. A timeout at one intermediate hop is not proof of a forwarding failure if later hops still respond.</li></ol><p class="lab-note">On Cisco IOS, pair host tests with <code>show ip interface brief</code>, <code>show interfaces</code>, and <code>show ip route</code>. Verify the fix with the original failing application, not just a successful ping.</p></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Build a Small Network</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
